WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.480
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.320
Today, with the power of computer&nbsp;
graphics research, we can use our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:14.480
computers to run fluid simulations, simulate&nbsp;
immersing a selection of objects into jelly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.320
or tear meat in a way that much like in&nbsp;
reality, it tears along the muscle fibers.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:27.120
If we look at the abstract of this amazing new&nbsp;
paper, we see this, quoting: “This allows us to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:32.560
trace high speed motion of objects colliding&nbsp;
against curved geometry, to reduce the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:38.400
of constraints, to increase the robustness of&nbsp;
the simulation, and to simplify the formulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:45.440
of the solver.” What! This sounds impossible,&nbsp;
but at the very least, outrageously good. Let’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.480
look at three examples of what it can do and see&nbsp;
for ourselves if it can live up to its promise.

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:55.600
One. It can simulate a steering&nbsp;
mechanism full of joints and contact.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:03.040
Yup, an entire servo steering mechanism is&nbsp;
simulated with a prescribed mass ratio. Loving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:09.040
it. I hereby declare that it passes inspection,&nbsp;
and now, we can take off for some off-roading.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:16.000
All of the movement is simulated really well,&nbsp;
and… wait a minute. Hold on to your papers!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.480
Are you seeing what I am seeing? Look. Even the&nbsp;
tire deformations are part of the simulation!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:29.040
Beautiful. And now, let’s do a stress test,&nbsp;
and race through a bunch of obstacles and see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.400
how well those tires can take it. At the end of&nbsp;
the video, I will tell you how much time it takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:40.720
to simulate all this…and note that I had to look&nbsp;
three times because I could not believe my eyes.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:48.320
Two. Restitution! Or in other words, we can smash&nbsp;
an independent marble into a bunch of others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:53.760
and their combined velocity will be correctly&nbsp;
computed. We know for a fact that the computations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:58.960
are correct, because when I stop the video&nbsp;
here, you can see that the marbles themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.360
are smiling. The joys of curved geometry and&nbsp;
specular reflections. Of course, this is not true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.760
because if we attempt to do the same with&nbsp;
a classical earlier technique by the name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.760 --> 00:02:16.000
position based dynamics, this would happen.&nbsp;
Yes, the velocities become erroneously large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.120
and the marbles jump off of the wire. And&nbsp;
they still appear to be very happy about it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:26.480
Of course, with the new technique, the&nbsp;
simulation is much more stable and realistic.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.120
Talking about stability. Is it stable&nbsp;
only in a small-scale simulation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.840
or can it take a huge scene with lots&nbsp;
of interactions? Would it still work?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:43.440
Let’s run a stress test and find out.&nbsp;
Ha-haa! This animation can run all day long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.440
and not one thing appears to&nbsp;
behave incorrectly. Loving this.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.440
Three. It can also simulate these beautiful&nbsp;
high-frequency rolls that we often experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:58.720
when we drop a coin on a table. This kind of&nbsp;
interaction is very challenging to simulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.320
correctly because of the high-frequency nature of&nbsp;
the motion and the curved geometry that interacts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:10.160
with the table. I would love to see a technique&nbsp;
that algorithmically generates the sound for this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.840
I could almost hear its sound&nbsp;
in my head! Believe it or not,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.560
this should be possible and is subject to&nbsp;
some research attention in computer graphics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:24.400
The animation was given, but the sounds&nbsp;
were algorithmically generated. Listen!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:33.760
Let me know in the comments if you are one&nbsp;
of our OG Fellow Scholars who were there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:36.560
when this episode was published&nbsp;
hundreds of videos ago.

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:42.400
So, how much do we have to wait to simulate&nbsp;
all of these crazy physical interactions?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:47.760
We mentioned that the tires are stiff and take a&nbsp;
great deal of computation to simulate properly.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:56.720
So, as always… all nighters, right? Nope! Look at&nbsp;
that! Holy mother of papers! The car example takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:03.600
only 18 milliseconds to compute per frame,&nbsp;
which means 55 frames per second. Goodness!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.800
Not only do we not need an all-nighter, we&nbsp;
don’t even need to leave for a coffee break!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:15.200
And the rolling marbles took even less, and,&nbsp;
wo-hoo! The high-frequency coin example needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:21.760
only one third of a millisecond, which means that&nbsp;
we can generate more than 3000 frames with it per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.200
second. We not only don’t need an all nighter or&nbsp;
a coffee break, we don’t even need to wait at all!

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:33.600
Now, at the start of the video, I noted that the&nbsp;
claim in the abstract sounds almost outrageous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:39.520
It is because it promises to be able to do more&nbsp;
than previous techniques, simplify the simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:47.120
algorithm itself, make it more robust, and do all&nbsp;
this while being blazing fast. If someone told me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.320
that there is a work that does all this at the&nbsp;
same time, I would say that give me that paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:58.240
immediately because I do not believe a word of&nbsp;
it. And yet, it really lives up to its promise.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.400
Typically, as a research field matures, we see new&nbsp;
techniques that can do more than previous methods,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:09.920
but the price to be paid for it is in the form&nbsp;
of complexity. The algorithms get more and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.840
involved over time, and with that, they often&nbsp;
get slower and less robust. The engineers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.280
industry have to decide how much complexity they&nbsp;
are willing to shoulder to be able to simulate all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.320
of these beautiful interactions. Don’t forget,&nbsp;
these code bases have to be maintained and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:33.760
improved for many-many years so choosing a simple&nbsp;
base algorithm is of utmost importance. But here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.840
none of these factors need to be considered,&nbsp;
because there is nearly no tradeoff here:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:42.720
it is simpler, more robust,&nbsp;
and better at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.160
It really feels like we are living in a&nbsp;
science fiction world. What a time to be alive!

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:53.120
Huge congratulations to scientists at NVIDIA&nbsp;
and the university of Copenhagen for this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.440
Don’t forget, they could have kept the results&nbsp;
for themselves, but they chose to share the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.800
details of this algorithm with everyone, free&nbsp;
of charge. Thank you so much for doing this.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:55.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

